/p>
<p>Hundreds of knights and nobles were in Bellacourt.</p>
<p>That gave Charles more trained fighters inside the capital.</p>
<p>It also meant their household troops were scattered across distant estates.</p>
<p>“A city full of knights,” Charles said bitterly, “and half their men are three days away.”</p>
<p>“Then we hold three days,” Alaric said.</p>
<p>Charles looked at him.</p>
<p>“Can we?”</p>
<p>Alaric studied the walls.</p>
<p>He had defended Castle Tarn against monsters.</p>
<p>He had fought in mountain passes, forests and ruins.</p>
<p>A human army was different.</p>
<p>Humans brought engineers.</p>
<p>Schedules.</p>
<p>Supply wagons.</p>
<p>Siege tools.</p>
<p>They also grew tired, argued and made mistakes.</p>
<p>“Bellacourt is strong,” Alaric said. “And Lothar moved quickly. Quick armies outrun things.”</p>
<p>“Such as?”</p>
<p>“Food. Heavy equipment. Reinforcements.”</p>
<p>Charles nodded.</p>
<p>That was the first useful thing anyone had said all morning.</p>
<p>By afternoon, Lothar closed the eastern road.</p>
<p>By evening, cavalry appeared south of the city.</p>
<p>By midnight, the northern gate was under observation.</p>
<p>Bellacourt was besieged.</p>
<p>The first assault came the next morning.</p>
<p>It was not the great attack Alaric had expected.</p>
<p>Lothar tested the walls.</p>
<p>Archers moved forward behind large shields. Infantry pushed ladders toward a lesser tower. Men with axes tried to damage an outer gate while cavalry remained beyond bowshot.</p>
<p>Charles’s defenders drove them back.</p>
<p>The city cheered.</p>
<p>Alaric did not.</p>
<p>“He wanted to see how quickly we reacted,” he said.</p>
<p>Raymond stood beside him on the wall.</p>
<p>“And now he knows.”</p>
<p>“Yes.”</p>
<p>“Comforting.”</p>
<p>The second day brought siege engines.</p>
<p>Not many.</p>
<p>Enough.</p>
<p>Small stone-throwers began striking the eastern wall. Work crews filled ditches beneath covering fire. Lothar’s men cut trees from nearby orchards and farms.</p>
<p>Bellacourt answered with bolts, stones, burning pitch and cavalry sorties through the western gate while the road remained partly open.</p>
<p>By the third day, even that road was threatened.</p>
<p>The siege tightened.</p>
<p>The city changed quickly.</p>
<p>Markets became ration points.</p>
<p>Guild halls became workshops for arrows, shields and repairs.</p>
<p>Monasteries became hospitals.</p>
<p>The same merchants Alaric had watched arguing over cloth prices now counted sacks of grain for the walls.</p>
<p>The same monks who had carried water west now carried wounded men through the streets.</p>
<p>The same systems he had spent days examining were suddenly under pressure.</p>
<p>Some bent.</p>
<p>Some failed.</p>
<p>Many worked.</p>
<p>At Notre-Dame du Lys, Archbishop Anselm ordered the cathedral crypts opened for civilians from damaged neighborhoods.</p>
<p>When a noble protested that his family should receive priority quarters, Anselm replied that stone did not recognize heraldry when it fell from the sky.</p>
<p>Alaric heard about it later and laughed for the first time in days.</p>
<p>On the fourth evening, Lothar sent a messenger.</p>
<p>The man rode beneath a white flag and asked to speak to Charles from beyond the east gate.</p>
<p>Charles went to the wall with Raymond, Alaric and Anselm.</p>
<p>Across the field stood a group of armored riders.</p>
<p>At their center was a broad-shouldered man in a dark cloak.</p>
<p>Warlord Lothar.</p>
<p>Beside him stood Count Bernier.</p>
<p>Bernier looked smaller than Alaric remembered.</p>
<p>Lothar raised his voice.</p>
<p>“Charles of Bretonnia!”</p>
<p>Charles stepped forward.</p>
<p>“I am here.”</p>
<p>“You have one day to open the gates.”</p>
<p>“And then?”</p>
<p>“Your soldiers lay down their arms. Your nobles swear new oaths. You retain your estates.”</p>
<p>Raymond muttered, “Generous.”</p>
<p>Lothar continued.</p>
<p>“Your cousin will be confirmed as heir under guarantees agreed with your lords.”</p>
<p>Raymond leaned over the wall.</p>
<p>“I am standing here!”</p>
<p>There was a brief silence across the battlefield.</p>
<p>Several defenders laughed.</p>
<p>Even Charles smiled.</p>
<p>Lothar did not.</p>
<p>Bernier looked away.</p>
<p>The failed kidnapping had reached him.</p>
<p>Alaric watched his face.</p>
<p>Bernier had expected to present Raymond as a captive piece on a political board.</p>
<p>Instead Raymond was shouting insults from a wall.</p>
<p>A major part of the political plan had failed.</p>
<p>But the army had been moving in parallel all along.</p>
<p>Charles called down.</p>
<p>“You crossed my borders under false orders, attacked my people and surrounded my capital. There will be no oath.”</p>
<p>Lothar’s answer was calm.</p>
<p>“Then the city pays for your pride.”</p>
<p>Bernier turned toward him sharply.</p>
<p>It was a small movement.</p>
<p>Alaric noticed.</p>
<p>The delegation rode away.</p>
<p>Anselm remained at the wall.</p>
<p>“He said the city pays.”</p>
<p>“Yes,” Charles said.</p>
<p>“He did not say the crown pays.”</p>
<p>“No.”</p>
<p>Bernier had heard it too.</p>
<p>That night the bombardment intensified.</p>
<p>A stone struck a house near the eastern gate and killed three people.</p>
<p>Another broke part of a tower stair.</p>
<p>Fires burned in two districts before guild bucket lines contained them.</p>
<p>Bellacourt could hold.</p>
<p>But not forever.</p>
<p>Charles’s outside armies were gathering.</p>
<p>Too slowly.</p>
<p>The nearest force strong enough to challenge Lothar was still days away.</p>
<p>And Lothar knew it.</p>
<p>Alaric stood over the map with Charles.</p>
<p>“There is one force that could arrive faster.”</p>
<p>Charles knew before he said it.</p>
<p>“The dragons.”</p>
<p>“The Valley.”</p>
<p>“Can they reach us?”</p>
<p>“If someone reaches them.”</p>
<p>They both looked toward the western road.</p>
<p>By then, Lothar’s scouts watched it from the hills.</p>
<p>The city was not completely sealed.</p>
<p>But leaving was nearly as dangerous as entering.</p>
<p>“We need a courier,” Charles said.</p>
<p>Raymond entered the room.</p>
<p>“I will go.”</p>
<p>“No,” Charles and Alaric said together.</p>
<p>Raymond looked offended.</p>
<p>Alaric pointed at him.</p>
<p>“We just recovered you.”</p>
<p>“This is becoming tiresome.”</p>
<p>“You are the heir.”</p>
<p>“I am also a rider.”</p>
<p>“An average one.”</p>
<p>Raymond stared.</p>
<p>“Cruel.”</p>
<p>Charles ignored them.</p>
<p>A young royal courier named Martin volunteered.</p>
<p>He knew the western farms, side roads and old hunting tracks better than any knight present.</p>
<p>The plan was simple.</p>
<p>At midnight, Bellacourt would launch a short cavalry sortie from the western gate.</p>
<p>Lothar’s scouts would move to intercept.</p>
<p>Martin would leave separately through a postern farther north, ride through vineyard paths and cross the river above the main road.</p>
<p>Then he would ride for the Valley.</p>
<p>Alaric gave him a small token of the Dragon Rider Order.</p>
<p>“Show this at Castle Tarn. Ask for Percival.”</p>
<p>Martin turned it over in his hand.</p>
<p>“And if they do not believe me?”</p>
<p>“Tell him Alaric says Vaelor still owes him three sheep.”</p>
<p>Martin frowned.</p>
<p>“Does he?”</p>
<p>“No.”</p>
<p>“Then why—”</p>
<p>“He will know I sent you.”</p>
<p>The courier smiled nervously.</p>
<p>At midnight the gates opened.</p>
<p>Hooves thundered into darkness.</p>
<p>Arrows answered from the hills.</p>
<p>For ten minutes the western fields became chaos.</p>
<p>Then the riders returned.</p>
<p>Three horses without riders.</p>
<p>Two wounded men.</p>
<p>No Martin.</p>
<p>No one knew whether that meant success or death.</p>
<p>Bellacourt could only wait.</p>
<h1>Chapter 10 – Breaking the Siege</h1>
<p>The fifth day passed.</p>
<p>Then the sixth.</p>
<p>Then the seventh.</p>
<p>No dragons came.</p>
<p>Lothar’s army built heavier engines.</p>
<p>The eastern wall cracked near one tower.</p>
<p>Defenders filled the gap with timber, carts, stones and anything else that could hold weight.</p>
<p>Food remained sufficient.</p>
<p>Arrows did not.</p>
<p>Sleep became something people borrowed in short pieces.</p>
<p>Alaric spent more time on the walls than in Côte de Lys.</p>
<p>Without Vaelor, he felt incomplete.</p>
<p>He hated admitting that.</p>
<p>For years, dragon and rider had fought as one unit.</p>
<p>Now every time Alaric looked over a wall, he saw the battlefield the way a dragon would see it.</p>
<p>Open fields.</p>
<p>Siege engines.</p>
<p>Supply wagons.</p>
<p>Dense formations.</p>
<p>All waiting for fire from above.</p>
<p>And above them was only sky.</p>
<p>On the seventh morning, Lothar attacked in force.</p>
<p>Drums began before sunrise.</p>
<p>Thousands of men moved toward the walls beneath banners Alaric did not recognize.</p>
<p>Archers darkened the air.</p>
<p>Ladders rose.</p>
<p>Siege engines struck the eastern defenses again and again.</p>
<p>At the damaged tower, part of the outer wall collapsed.</p>
<p>The breach was not wide.</p>
<p>It was wide enough.</p>
<p>Lothar’s infantry rushed it.</p>
<p>Alaric met them there.</p>
<p>For an hour the battle became stone, mud, smoke and steel.</p>
<p>There was no room for elegant swordsmanship.</p>
<p>Men pushed shields against shields.</p>
<p>Spears stabbed through gaps.</p>
<p>Defenders hauled wounded men backward while fresh soldiers took their places.</p>
<p>Alaric fought beside Bretonnian knights who days earlier had laughed at the Dragon Riders’ poor jousting.</p>
<p>Now no one cared how anyone sat a horse.</p>
<p>Master Bertrand appeared beside him with blood on one sleeve.</p>
<p>“How is the riding?” he shouted.</p>
<p>Alaric blocked a spear.</p>
<p>“Improving!”</p>
<p>“Good!”</p>
<p>Then Bertrand shoved a man off the rubble with his shield.</p>
<p>The defenders held the breach.</p>
<p>Barely.</p>
<p>By afternoon, Lothar withdrew.</p>
<p>The field outside Bellacourt was covered with broken ladders, abandoned shields and wounded men from both sides.</p>
<p>The city bells rang.</p>
<p>Not victory.</p>
<p>Warning.</p>
<p>Because everyone knew he would attack again.</p>
<p>In Lothar’s camp, Bernier finally argued openly with him.</p>
<p>Alaric did not hear the conversation.</p>
<p>Later, prisoners described it.</p>
<p>Bernier demanded that the city be spared once the walls fell.</p>
<p>Lothar asked why.</p>
<p>“Because it is the capital,” Bernier said.</p>
<p>“Exactly,” Lothar answered.</p>
<p>A city that resisted had to learn what resistance cost.</p>
<p>Bernier had imagined a change of rulers, alliances and influence.</p>
<p>Lothar saw wealth, submission and fear.</p>
<p>By then, Bernier understood the difference.</p>
<p>Too late.</p>
<p>The eighth day passed without relief.</p>
<p>Charles joined Alaric on the wall.</p>
<p>“You think the courier failed.”</p>
<p>“I think nothing.”</p>
<p>“That is unlike you.”</p>
<p>“I am trying something new.”</p>
<p>Charles looked over the fields.</p>
<p>Lothar’s men were preparing another assault.</p>
<p>“Would Vaelor come?”</p>
<p>Alaric turned toward him.</p>
<p>“If he knows?”</p>
<p>“Yes.”</p>
<p>“Nothing could stop him.”</p>
<p>Charles nodded.</p>
<p>“That is almost reassuring.”</p>
<p>Below them, people moved through the streets carrying water and stones.</p>
<p>A group of monks helped reinforce a gate.</p>
<p>Guild apprentices carried bundles of arrows from workshops.</p>
<p>Noble retainers hauled carts beside fishermen and bakers.</p>
<p>The estates, guilds and institutions of Bretonnia had become less distinct under siege.</p>
<p>Not gone.</p>
<p>But rearranged by necessity.</p>
<p>Anselm approached the wall.</p>
<p>He carried no ceremonial robes now.</p>
<p>Only a dark cloak over simple armor someone had convinced him to wear.</p>
<p>“The hospital crypts are full,” he said. “We can open the eastern chapter house if needed.”</p>
<p>Charles nodded.</p>
<p>Anselm looked at Alaric.</p>
<p>“Still nothing?”</p>
<p>“No.”</p>
<p>The Archbishop looked toward the west.</p>
<p>Then toward the enemy camp.</p>
<p>“I find myself hoping for dragons.”</p>
<p>Alaric smiled faintly.</p>
<p>“Careful.”</p>
<p>“I said hoping. Not approving.”</p>
<p>“Of course.”</p>
<p>On the ninth day the drums began again.</p>
<p>This assault was larger than the first.</p>
<p>Lothar had moved engines close to the walls overnight.</p>
<p>Under heavy covering fire, men advanced with shields, ladders and hooked poles.</p>
<p>At the eastern breach, defenders braced behind new timber barricades.</p>
<p>At the south gate, another force pushed a ram forward beneath a wet hide roof.</p>
<p>Alaric ran between positions until he no longer knew which direction the city lay.</p>
<p>The south gate shook.</p>
<p>Once.</p>
<p>Twice.</p>
<p>Again.</p>
<p>The hinges held.</p>
<p>A fourth strike splintered part of the inner beam.</p>
<p>Raymond stood above the gatehouse throwing stones down with everyone else.</p>
<p>“Still think I should stay inside the citadel?”</p>
<p>“Yes!” Alaric shouted.</p>
<p>“Wonderful!”</p>
<p>Another blow struck the gate.</p>
<p>Then a sound rolled across the city.</p>
<p>Deep.</p>
<p>Distant.</p>
<p>Not a bell.</p>
<p>Not thunder.</p>
<p>Alaric stopped moving.</p>
<p>Around him, men continued fighting.</p>
<p>No one else understood yet.</p>
<p>The sound came again.</p>
<p>A roar.</p>
<p>Alaric looked west.</p>
<p>At first he saw nothing.</p>
<p>Then a black shape crossed the sun.</p>
<p>Another followed.</p>
<p>Then five.</p>
<p>Then twelve.</p>
<p>More appeared behind them.</p>
<p>The sky filled with wings.</p>
<p>Someone on the western wall shouted.</p>
<p>The cry spread faster than fire.</p>
<p>“DRAGONS!”</p>
<p>Lothar’s soldiers heard it too.</p>
<p>The attack faltered.</p>
<p>Men looked upward.</p>
<p>For most of them, dragons belonged to stories.</p>
<p>For some, monsters.</p>
<p>For a few, gods.</p>
<p>None had expected them to arrive as a disciplined army.</p>
<p>At the front flew a sapphire-blue dragon.</p>
<p>Vaelor.</p>
<p>Alaric laughed aloud.</p>
<p>Vaelor folded his wings and descended.</p>
<p>Behind him came dragons of red, green, silver and colors Alaric could not see through the smoke.</p>
<p>Dragon Rider Knights filled the sky above Bellacourt.</p>
<p>Not all sixty.</p>
<p>Enough.</p>
<p>The first pass struck Lothar’s siege engines.</p>
<p>Dragons did not burn the infantry packed closest to the walls.</p>
<p>The riders had chosen their targets carefully.</p>
<p>Fire swept across abandoned engine positions, timber towers, supply wagons and ammunition stores after warning passes scattered the men around them.</p>
<p>A stone-thrower vanished beneath a burst of flame.</p>
<p>Another dragon shattered the roof over the south gate’s battering ram with a controlled blast.</p>
<p>The soldiers carrying it dropped the beam and ran.</p>
<p>Bellacourt erupted in cheers.</p>
<p>Then the gates opened.</p>
<p>Charles had been waiting for that moment.</p>
<p>Bretonnian cavalry poured out from the western and northern gates while Lothar’s formations struggled to face two directions at once.</p>
<p>The Dragon Riders attacked from above.</p>
<p>Charles’s knights struck from the city.</p>
<p>Outside forces that had been gathering beyond the siege lines finally advanced as well, seeing the signal and hearing the horns.</p>
<p>Lothar’s carefully organized army began to come apart.</p>
<p>Alaric ran toward the inner court.</p>
<p>Vaelor landed hard enough to scatter dust across the stones.</p>
<p>Alaric stopped in front of him.</p>
<p>For one second neither moved.</p>
<p>Then Vaelor lowered his head.</p>
<p>Alaric pressed his forehead against the dragon’s scales.</p>
<p>“You took your time.”</p>
<p>Vaelor growled.</p>
<p>Percival slid from his dragon’s saddle.</p>
<p>“Martin reached us two days ago.”</p>
<p>“He made it?”</p>
<p>“Alive. Barely. We gathered the nearest riders and flew as soon as the dragons were ready.”</p>
<p>Alaric exhaled.</p>
<p>Percival looked toward the battle.</p>
<p>“Do you intend to stand here?”</p>
<p>“No.”</p>
<p>Alaric climbed into Vaelor’s saddle.</p>
<p>The world changed immediately.</p>
<p>The courtyard fell away.</p>
<p>Walls became lines.</p>
<p>Streets became patterns.</p>
<p>The enemy camp spread beneath him.</p>
<p>He could see Lothar’s reserves moving east.</p>
<p>He could see supply wagons trying to escape.</p>
<p>He could see Bernier’s banners near the command tents.</p>
<p>“Vaelor,” Alaric said.</p>
<p>The dragon banked.</p>
<p>They struck the camp.</p>
<p>Not the tents themselves.</p>
<p>The road beyond them.</p>
<p>Vaelor’s fire cut across a line of wagons and forced Lothar’s retreating cavalry away from the northern route.</p>
<p>Other dragons descended in coordinated passes.</p>
<p>The siege became a rout.</p>
<p>By sunset, Lothar’s army was retreating east.</p>
<p>Not destroyed.</p>
<p>Broken enough.</p>
<p>Bernier did not escape with the first wave.</p>
<p>Royal cavalry found him near an abandoned command wagon with a handful of retainers.</p>
<p>He surrendered.</p>
<p>When Charles saw him later, bound but unharmed, Bernier looked less like the elegant court conspirator from the feast and more like a tired old man.</p>
<p>“You brought him here,” Charles said.</p>
<p>Bernier did not answer.</p>
<p>“You opened roads to him. Delayed warnings. Lied to men who trusted seals bearing my name.”</p>
<p>“I did.”</p>
<p>“And then discovered he did not intend to make you his partner.”</p>
<p>Bernier looked toward the smoke outside the walls.</p>
<p>“No.”</p>
<p>Charles’s expression hardened.</p>
<p>“That discovery does not absolve you.”</p>
<p>“I know.”</p>
<p>His final judgment would come later.</p>
<p>For now there were wounded to count, fires to extinguish and walls to repair.</p>
<p>As evening fell, the dragons landed outside Bellacourt.</p>
<p>The reaction was not what Alaric expected.</p>
<p>People came out slowly at first.</p>
<p>Soldiers.</p>
<p>Then children.</p>
<p>Then families who had spent days